.TH HTTP_RECV 3
.SH NAME
http_recv \- read the decoded body of a response
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <http.h>
.P
.BI "ssize_t http_recv(http* " h ", char* " buf ", size_t " len ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR http_recv ()
stores up to
.I len
bytes of the body of the current response in
.IR buf .
It works on what
.BR http_canread (3)
has put into the input buffer and never reads the socket, so it does not block and is cheap to call
again. It decodes the framing of the response:
.TP
HTTP_TRANSFER_CHUNKED
the chunk sizes, the line ends between chunks and the trailers are removed; a
size with extensions ("5;ext=1") is understood.
.TP
HTTP_TRANSFER_LENGTH
as many bytes as Content-Length says, bytes behind them are left in the buffer.
.TP
HTTP_TRANSFER_UNDEF
everything until the connection closes.
.P
When the last byte of the body has been taken the status of the response becomes
HTTP_STATUS_FINISH. The call can return fewer bytes than are available if a chunk boundary
or an incomplete chunk size line is in the way; call it again after the next
.BR http_canread (3).
.I h->stream
must be set before the request is sent, otherwise
.BR http_canread (3)
moves the body away.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The number of bytes stored, 0 if there is nothing for now (the body is complete when response->status is HTTP_STATUS_FINISH), -1 on an error: errno is EPIPE when a chunked body or a body with a length was cut off by the closing of the connection, EPROTO for a damaged chunk size, otherwise the error of the connection.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
while((n = http_recv(&h, buf, sizeof(buf))) > 0)
  sse_feed(&parser, buf, n, on_event, 0);

if(n < 0)
  errmsg_warnsys("answer cut off", 0);
else if(h.response->status == HTTP_STATUS_FINISH)
  ...done...
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
http(3), http_canread(3), sse_feed(3), ndjson_feed(3)
